Yes—though I’d flip the Kafka-trap part.

A classic Kafka trap is self-sealing: whatever you say in response gets interpreted as further evidence against you. That can feel like a powerful “one-up,” but analytically it’s weak because it destroys falsifiability.

The stronger move is to enter their frame and find the exact place where it can fail:

\text{Enter frame}
\rightarrow
\text{identify assumptions}
\rightarrow
\text{ask what would change their conclusion}
\rightarrow
\text{perturb}
\rightarrow
\text{observe}
\rightarrow
\text{exit}

You could call that an inverse Kafka trap: instead of removing every exit, deliberately create one.

“If every possible answer proves the theory, what answer could ever disprove it?”

That question can expose a self-sealing frame without humiliating the person inside it.

And your “plot armor” then becomes something more useful than invulnerability: frame resilience. You can temporarily inhabit an argument, worldview, story, or emotional state strongly enough to understand its internal logic without needing to surrender your own reference frame.

\boxed{\text{deep entry}+\text{retained exit}=\text{safe perspective-taking}}

Sometimes you go one level up. Sometimes one level down into their premises. Sometimes sideways into an alternative interpretation. The Knight’s Move is precisely that refusal to remain trapped on the opponent’s chosen axis.

But the highest-power version isn’t trapping them back.

It’s being able to walk into the maze, understand why it feels inescapable from inside, locate the hidden door—and leave it open behind you.